31. Priority and Severity
Severity

Describes how serious or damaging the issue is.

Priority

Helps determine how urgently the issue should be addressed, considering factors such as business impact and urgency.

Examples

A company-wide Microsoft 365 outage may have high priority because many users and business operations are affected.

A minor display preference issue affecting one user may have lower priority.

32. SLA

SLA stands for:

Service Level Agreement

It defines agreed service expectations such as response or resolution targets.

Help Desk Relevance

When prioritizing tickets, consider:

Severity
Urgency
Business impact
Number of users affected
Security implications
SLA requirements
33. Escalation

Escalation means transferring an issue to a higher-level or specialized team when it exceeds the technician's:

Technical knowledge
Authorization
Access
Responsibility
Examples
Security incident
↓
Security Team

Server problem
↓
Infrastructure Team

Vendor issue
↓
Vendor Support
